  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $E?$\n\nI. $F∧(F∨E)$\n\nII. $E∧(F∨E)$\n\nIII. $E∨(F∨E)$",
  "worked_solution": "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nE∧(E∨F)≡E.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F∧(F∨E) & ≡F≢E.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E∧(F∨E) & ≡E∧(E∨F) \\\\  & ≡E.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
